1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
You like living in New York, don't you?

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:02,978
Oh, I love it.

3
00:00:02,978 --> 00:00:04,978
It's so convenient.

4
00:00:04,978 --> 00:00:06,540
I can take the bus to work ...

5
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:07,416
or the subway ...

6
00:00:07,416 --> 00:00:08,292
or a taxi.

7
00:00:08,292 --> 00:00:10,434
And there's so much to do.

8
00:00:10,434 --> 00:00:12,912
Lots of movie houses, and the theater.

9
00:00:12,912 --> 00:00:14,890
I know what you mean.

10
00:00:14,890 --> 00:00:16,640
I'd like to live in the city,

11
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:19,008
but living in New Jersey and the suburbs

12
00:00:19,008 --> 00:00:20,148
is better for Michelle.

13
00:00:20,148 --> 00:00:20,846
Trees, grass.

14
00:00:20,846 --> 00:00:25,140
There's a lot of good things about suburban living.

15
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:27,778
I grew up in Riverdale, remember?

16
00:00:27,778 --> 00:00:29,242
So I know.

17
00:00:29,242 --> 00:00:31,104
But, as a working woman,

18
00:00:31,104 --> 00:00:35,998
I think New York has all the conveniences--

19
00:00:35,998 --> 00:00:38,714
including the best tomatoes.

20
00:00:38,714 --> 00:00:43,402
The truth is, I'd like to live in the city.

21
00:00:43,402 --> 00:00:45,682
Michelle's the right age.

22
00:00:45,682 --> 00:00:46,664
There are lots of things for her here.

23
00:00:46,664 --> 00:00:48,357
You're right, Harry.

24
00:00:48,357 --> 00:00:50,132
Today is the perfect example.

25
00:00:50,132 --> 00:00:53,325
Michelle and her friends are at the aquarium in Brooklyn.

26
00:00:53,325 --> 00:00:55,247
They come back here for lunch,

27
00:00:55,247 --> 00:00:58,981
then go uptown to the Museum of Natural History.

28
00:00:58,981 --> 00:01:01,226
There's so much for young people to see and do.

29
00:01:01,226 --> 00:01:02,226
It's just incredible!

30
00:01:02,226 --> 00:01:03,650
Not just for young people.

31
00:01:03,650 --> 00:01:04,484
What about me?

32
00:01:04,484 --> 00:01:06,043
I've never been to the aquarium

33
00:01:06,043 --> 00:01:07,627
or the Museum of Natural History.

34
00:01:07,627 --> 00:01:08,493
Have you?

35
00:01:08,493 --> 00:01:09,300
Oh yes, Harry.

36
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:10,377
My mother and father

37
00:01:10,377 --> 00:01:12,471
often took us somewhere in the city on the weekends.

38
00:01:12,471 --> 00:01:14,449
Dad was a busy doctor,

39
00:01:14,449 --> 00:01:16,871
but he usually managed to squeeze a Sunday in

40
00:01:16,871 --> 00:01:18,687
with Richard, Robbie, and me.

41
00:01:18,687 --> 00:01:23,758
I used to love to go to the Metropolitan Museum of Art.

42
00:01:23,758 --> 00:01:25,633
I've been there several times.

43
00:01:25,633 --> 00:01:26,592
Twice with Michelle.

44
00:01:26,592 --> 00:01:28,899
You ought to think about spending more time with Michelle

45
00:01:28,899 --> 00:01:33,517
at all the great places in the city.

46
00:01:33,517 --> 00:01:34,117
Well, perhaps,

47
00:01:34,117 --> 00:01:41,251
you'll help me select some of the great places.

48
00:01:41,251 --> 00:01:48,707
And perhaps, you'll join us?

49
00:01:48,707 --> 00:01:50,545
Perhaps I will.

50
00:01:50,545 --> 00:01:52,105
Well, there we are.

51
00:01:52,105 --> 00:01:53,163
All set for lunch, Harry.

52
00:01:53,163 --> 00:01:54,470
It looks inviting.

53
00:01:54,470 --> 00:01:56,820
I wish Michelle and her friends would get here.

54
00:01:56,820 --> 00:01:57,727
I'm starving, aren't you?

55
00:01:57,727 --> 00:02:03,096
I can't wait to take a bite of the pumpernickel.

56
00:02:03,096 --> 00:02:08,052
It smells so delicious.

57
00:02:08,052 --> 00:02:09,312
Coming! Coming!

58
00:02:09,312 --> 00:02:10,844
Who is it?

59
00:02:10,844 --> 00:02:11,116
Michelle.

60
00:02:11,116 --> 00:02:11,316
It's us, Susan.

61
00:02:11,116 --> 00:02:11,828
Come in, Michelle,

62
00:02:11,828 --> 00:02:13,158
and bring your friends along.

63
00:02:13,158 --> 00:02:15,008
We're on the fifth floor.

64
00:02:15,008 --> 00:02:23,242
Oh, you've been here before.

65
00:02:23,242 --> 00:02:23,542
Susan,

66
00:02:23,542 --> 00:02:24,442
I really appreciate

67
00:02:24,442 --> 00:02:26,920
your doing this for Michelle and her friends.

68
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:27,976
Oh, please, Harry.

69
00:02:27,976 --> 00:02:28,898
It's nothing.

70
00:02:28,898 --> 00:02:31,876
I'm not just doing it for Michelle.

71
00:02:31,876 --> 00:02:34,854
I'm doing it for you.

72
00:02:34,854 --> 00:02:37,832
Thank you.

73
00:02:37,832 --> 00:02:43,788
I'm doing it for us, Harry.

74
00:02:43,788 --> 00:02:44,388
Well,

75
00:02:44,388 --> 00:02:50,336
it's ... it's important for Michelle to see us together more often.

76
00:02:50,336 --> 00:02:50,536
That's true.

77
00:02:50,336 --> 00:02:50,536
It's important

78
00:02:50,494 --> 00:02:55,989
for Michelle and me to get to know each other better.

79
00:02:55,989 --> 00:02:59,110
That's important for us.

80
00:02:59,110 --> 00:03:02,022
That makes me feel good.

81
00:03:02,022 --> 00:03:02,753
What, Harry?

82
00:03:02,753 --> 00:03:04,884
What makes you feel good?

83
00:03:04,884 --> 00:03:06,384
Well, that you care about Michelle,

84
00:03:06,384 --> 00:03:08,072
that you care about me, and

85
00:03:08,072 --> 00:03:10,589
that you care about us.

86
00:03:10,589 --> 00:03:15,567
Well, Harry, that's because I do.

87
00:03:15,567 --> 00:03:19,523
I do care.

88
00:03:19,523 --> 00:03:22,501
They'll be here any minute.

89
00:03:22,501 --> 00:03:28,457
Susan, I'd like to continue this conversation later.

90
00:03:28,457 --> 00:03:31,435
Of course, Harry.

91
00:03:31,435 --> 00:03:34,413
We'll finish the conversation when they go to the museum.

92
00:03:34,413 --> 00:03:37,391
I'd like that.

93
00:03:37,391 --> 00:03:40,391
So would I.
